AB  - GitHub Actions (GHA) has become one of the most widely adopted CI/CD platforms in DevOps ecosystems. While GitHub-hosted runners offer standardized and controlled execution environments, many projects employ self-hosted runners to meet customized operational requirements, which unfortunately introduce new vectors for security compromise. Existing detection tools often fail to identify these risks accurately. To address this gap, this paper presents SHCRGuard, a novel framework for detecting and analyzing security risks in GHA workflows that employ self-hosted runners. SHCRGuard enhances static abstract syntax tree (AST) analysis with improved variable resolution for precise data tracing and integrates large language model (LLM)-assisted reasoning only as an optional fallback for rare cases where static analysis cannot produce a definitive result. It further incorporates four threat classification modules that operationalize the defined threat model for systematic risk evaluation. We assess SHCRGuard using a large-scale dataset of 1925 real-world GHA self-hosted workflow jobs. Experimental results show that SHCRGuard outperforms the state-of-the-art tool GHAST in detecting self-hosted runners, achieving a macro-F1 of 94.3%. Further analysis reveals that 1/3 of the corresponding CI/CD jobs exhibit at least one security alert, and 31.18% of them contain multiple alerts. Besides, 99.12% of the corresponding CI/CD jobs were found to have at least one kind of security risk, with permission-related flaws being the most severe and widespread. These findings call for tailored defense strategies to mitigate systemic risks in self-hosted runner environments.
