AB  - This chapter bridges the gap between running Kubeflow locally and operating a production machine learning (ML) platform that teams actually trust. Moving beyond basic Kubeflow setup, it addresses the critical operational challenges that determine whether an ML platform succeeds or becomes abandoned infrastructure: storage integration, continuous deployment, monitoring, multitenancy, and cost management. The chapter begins with production-grade S3 storage integration, demonstrating modern authentication patterns using IAM Roles for Service Accounts (IRSA) that eliminate hardcoded credentials while enabling fine-grained access control. Readers learn to configure Kubeflow components, i.e., pipelines, notebooks, and model serving, to securely access cloud storage with proper credential management and troubleshooting strategies for common authentication failures. CI/CD integration transforms pipeline development from manual, error-prone deployments to automated, traceable workflows. Using GitHub Actions as a practical reference implementation, the chapter shows how to build validation pipelines that catch errors before production, implement environment-specific configurations, and maintain deployment audit trails. Complete workflow examples demonstrate compilation testing, artifact management, and automated deployment to Kubeflow clusters. The monitoring and observability section establishes production-grade visibility using Prometheus and Grafana. Beyond basic metrics collection, it covers designing meaningful dashboards for ML workloads, implementing cost tracking through custom recording rules, and setting up alert routing that ensures the right notifications reach the right people. Real-world examples include monitoring GPU utilization, tracking pipeline execution patterns, and calculating per-team infrastructure costs. Multitenant setup enables multiple teams to share cluster resources safely through Kubernetes namespaces, resource quotas, and network policies. The chapter demonstrates profile-based isolation, quota design strategies that balance efficiency with guaranteed resources, and network policy patterns that prevent cross-team interference while allowing necessary service communication. Practical examples show intentional CPU overcommitment versus strict GPU allocation. Cost management strategies address the financial reality of running ML infrastructure. Readers learn to implement resource quotas that prevent runaway costs, design autoscaling policies for training workloads, optimize storage lifecycle management, and build showback reports that attribute costs to teams. The section includes concrete tactics for GPU cost optimization and techniques for identifying resource waste. The chapter concludes with platform design decisions, comparing managed services versus self-hosted deployments, evaluating cloud provider options, and discussing the operational trade-offs of different architectural choices. Throughout, the emphasis remains on practical, production-tested patterns rather than theoretical ideals, helping platform engineers build ML infrastructure that scales with organizational needs while remaining maintainable by real teams.
